.TH VERIFY_MD5 1 "2026-08-21" "diffvim 2.0" "diffvim scripts"
.SH NAME
verify_md5 \- 
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B verify_md5
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIargs\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
NAME\fR
    verify_md5.sh — Round-trip MD5 verification of the diffvim pipelines\fR
\fR
SYNOPSIS\fR
    verify_md5.sh [-h|--help]\fR
\fR
DESCRIPTION\fR
    For every example pair under examples/ (matching [0-9]*_*), runs\fR
    TWO diffvim pipelines in parallel (8 concurrent via xargs -P):\fR
\fR
      1. C pipeline:        compute-cpp → C postprocess → C pace → C animator\fR
                            (via animator/diffvim-pipeline --no-display ...)\fR
      2. Pure-Perl pipeline: compute_builtin.pl → postprocess.pl →\fR
                            pace.pl → animator.pl\fR
\fR
    Both pipelines write their final buffer to a snapshot file, the\fR
    MD5 of each snapshot is computed, and those MD5s are compared to\fR
    the MD5 of the expected new file. The script prints a table with\fR
    one row per example showing: example name, new-file MD5, C-pipeline\fR
    MD5, and Perl-pipeline MD5, followed by a summary count of\fR
    OK/bad for each pipeline.\fR
\fR
    This verifies that:\fR
      1. The C pipeline produces the correct output (its buffer's MD5\fR
         matches the new file's MD5).\fR
      2. The Perl pipeline produces byte-identical output to the C\fR
         pipeline (so the Perl tools are first-class, not just a\fR
         fallback).\fR
\fR
OPTIONS\fR
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show detailed help and exit
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR diffvim (1)
